  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 230 AM MST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\nSeasonally typical temperatures are expected to last through the end \nof the work week. While we will still see the atmosphere attempt to \nbuild the ridge back in, model guidance is in good agreement showing \nanother vort-max / shortwave trough moving through the region \nThursday into Friday. Only a small handful of ensemble members have \nany precipitation whatsoever with this system, it will prevent a \nmore significant warming trend from taking hold, and perhaps bring a \nbit more cloud cover to the area Thursday night or Friday.\n\nEnsembles are in excellent agreement showing cleaner ridging taking \nover by Sunday over the region. NAEFS mean 700-mb temperatures spike \nabove +6C, which exceeds the climatological 90th percentile. After \nthis, most guidance shows the ridge flattening out a bit, but with \ntemperatures remaining above to well above average for this time of \nyear. Highs are expected to climb back to the mid to upper 50s \naround Rawlins and Laramie, and into the 60s east of the Laramie \nrange. Values should be about 10F above average Sunday onwards. Even \na wind event will be hard to come by, with fewer than 20% of \nensembles supporting elevated winds during the next week. \nPrecipitation chances remain largely minimal for the remainder \nof the operational forecast period. Since the snow did not \nmanage to stick in Cheyenne again this morning, this outlook all\nbut guarantees a top-10 latest first measurable snowfall for \nthe city this year. In the very long-term beyond the end of the \noperational forecast period, there are some indications in large\nensemble means for the long- wave Pacific trough to move inland\nand open the door to increased storminess across the West \naround November 13-16.",
